================================================================================

This file exists to draw an explicit line between (a) established public
techniques this project applies, which are not owned by anyone and are not the
subject of any copyright or authorship claim here, and (b) this project's own
original contribution: the specific architecture, rule corpus, DSL, and
documentation, which are original expression authored for PORTCULLIS.

Methods, algorithms and architectural patterns are not copyrightable subject
matter in the first place (17 U.S.C. §102(b) and equivalent provisions
elsewhere) — copyright protects the particular expression of an idea, not the
idea itself. The attributions below are provided in that spirit: as a record
of intellectual debt and good scholarly practice, not because attribution is
legally required for using a public, decades-old technique.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Techniques used, and their origin
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MinHash / locality-sensitive hashing for near-duplicate detection
    Broder, A.Z. (1997). "On the resemblance and containment of documents."
    Compression and Complexity of Sequences. Public, peer-reviewed technique
    in general academic use for over two decades; used here (planned, M3) for
    corpus deduplication prior to the family-disjoint split.

Cascaded / staged classification with cost-ordered short-circuiting
    General pattern in both classical ML (e.g. Viola & Jones, 2001, "Rapid
    Object Detection using a Boosted Cascade of Simple Features") and modern
    security tooling (WAFs, EDR pipelines). PORTCULLIS's specific five-layer
    cascade (L0-L5), its layer boundaries, its per-layer contract, and its
    fusion approach are this project's own design — see docs/adr/0001.

Signature/rule engines over regular expressions with a small combinator DSL
    General pattern shared by YARA, Sigma, and Semgrep, among others.
    PORTCULLIS's specific DSL (`regex`/`all`/`any`/`not`/`near`), its rule
    schema, and its 50-rule corpus in rules/ are original work authored for
    this project — no rule text, pattern, or schema was copied from any of
    the tools named above.

Unicode NFKC normalisation, confusable-character folding, zero-width and
bidirectional control stripping
    NFKC is a published Unicode Consortium normalisation form (Unicode
    Standard Annex #15). The confusables concept is documented in Unicode
    Technical Standard #39. PORTCULLIS's confusables table
    (packages/core/src/portcullis/core/l0/confusables.py) is a small,
    hand-curated, independently authored subset — not a copy of the Unicode
    Consortium's confusables.txt data file.

Noisy-OR aggregation of independent weak signals into a bounded score
    Standard technique in Bayesian probability and expert-systems literature;
    no single origin, in general public use since at least the 1980s.

Platt scaling / isotonic regression for probability calibration (planned, M4)
    Platt, J. (1999); Zadrozny & Elkan (2002). Standard scikit-learn-supported
    calibration methods, applied here to this project's own fusion output.

